#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083245 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083057 :question/id #uuid "db8c5a47-27b8-548d-8bd7-65289cff06da" :question/math-academy-id "e-598" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9dcc4c97bd296ef4627b02b6cfafcdb643f475cd8dacd36a820db760bd6fb045.png)\n\nThe following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1, \\\\  \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right).$ For this, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}+4(0)}{2(0-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form \n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2,\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2) & =-2(x-(\\sqrt{2}+1)) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+2 & =-2x+2\\sqrt{2}+2 \\\\ y+2x & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aa64069-1206-5421-8a4f-a6e818653412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{5}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080166} {:answer/id #uuid "25e79155-ec81-5750-8024-8b85f305474b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{12}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080167} {:answer/id #uuid "287cb0ff-c745-5fe1-847e-2f4eb5158055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{8}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080168} {:answer/id #uuid "52f88dfd-78ed-5cbe-9062-4303b028313d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{7}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080169} {:answer/id #uuid "571c9ee7-eb34-5dec-8694-3d455d4bf27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080170}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c73154-e807-5407-a20e-cfe049bc0a94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080165}] :question/id #uuid "8def6986-b021-50a6-91db-c2da4068ed38" :question/math-academy-id "q-15558" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d40bd1a54762b360dc590f014d1df9731168f8620df07ee8f0294bd078fc8da5.png)\n\nThe curve shown above is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = t^{4} + t, y = t^{2}, - ∞ < t < ∞$.\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t =-1$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1129c14f-0c46-52c4-9b9a-b4200ce4b285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272542} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5faf98-bf15-5a2b-9fdc-09a9a727c6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273199} {:answer/id #uuid "5f37ca8d-3ea5-5dc1-85e2-3a3270cb4ca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273206} {:answer/id #uuid "7e745226-ecb3-5bd0-91a4-532e194cf6a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273471} {:answer/id #uuid "818af87a-0a7b-5074-95df-e9381f58f773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1129c14f-0c46-52c4-9b9a-b4200ce4b285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272542} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3ec910-ef36-5928-896c-acb859df3e11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274961}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbab7bcf-6a8b-5a33-ae85-61d0acf13fd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-15570" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\pi ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f344a5d-f20b-5d29-bc4b-455872b79c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254087} {:answer/id #uuid "9e617510-b480-5d70-b155-ca6d7883d5d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255062} {:answer/id #uuid "b77a05d6-9403-5a91-8f6f-1d9bf6e028b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255297} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa266e3-b7f3-5389-b0e2-d0701e510855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{3}x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255519} {:answer/id #uuid "d0067f8f-a0ec-5d6c-b01d-2fd5c88ca389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0067f8f-a0ec-5d6c-b01d-2fd5c88ca389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255523} :answer-field/id #uuid "38fc7afa-faeb-58be-bde2-8b46b42e1581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256025}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45f7632b-4b69-541a-a152-af5eeab4cd2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122898" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-3t\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,-1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-3}{\\frac{2}{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{3}-3t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{3}-3(1)}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(\\frac{3}{2})\\,}=-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,-1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9706bfa8-3bb2-557b-b07a-3cb1417d9a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9706bfa8-3bb2-557b-b07a-3cb1417d9a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318080} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c918410-e361-5bcd-9d06-8fb2ed574022" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318509} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9f0baf7-6810-54d5-a562-b853fd4577c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9f0baf7-6810-54d5-a562-b853fd4577c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318344} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d59423d-64dc-523d-9ab7-74063ac68307" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318661}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12a180e2-5235-5f82-b761-d705d49812b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-350386" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}=1-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}+7(-1)^{2}+8(-1)=-1+7-8=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(1,-2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}+14t+8}{2t+\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}+14(-1)+8}{2(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,-2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal intersects the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) & =2 \\\\ x-1 & =-4 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(-3,\\,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a237665-5e72-5ef0-972a-daed0f365535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a237665-5e72-5ef0-972a-daed0f365535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318093} :answer-field/id #uuid "429e4c9a-9cfb-50e6-a993-4807d39b6109" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318565} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24461eb9-a35c-56e6-b177-bda2a062fa02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24461eb9-a35c-56e6-b177-bda2a062fa02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317678} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e11efaf-e900-5155-8422-a40a0e3088ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318769}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe83c43c-ad52-5fa5-817f-abc0952cf819" :question/math-academy-id "q-350395" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7{t}^{2}-9t,\\,y={t}^{6}+2{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7({1}^{2})-9(1)=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{6}+2({1}^{2})=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,3).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=14t-9,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{5}+4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{5}+4t}{14t-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{6({1}^{5})+4(1)}{14(1)-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,3)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125bc6a6-fb74-5cce-8504-caeb0db01da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333153} {:answer/id #uuid "8002ae58-b098-5bda-ad00-0c7449da285b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334127} {:answer/id #uuid "88ce2a6a-ee62-5f62-9806-39eacf4d3091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{9}{4}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334202} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd1a297-06d6-51de-b3b8-df8da09b3b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{12}{5}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334652} {:answer/id #uuid "ea795a17-22c8-5438-a337-67f964713d05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "125bc6a6-fb74-5cce-8504-caeb0db01da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333153} :answer-field/id #uuid "48542b45-fcfe-5b1a-aede-206d84c3eeaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335378}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dafa44c9-9831-51c8-95fc-4a16466f716d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6858" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=2-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={0}^{3}-3\\cdot 0=0-0=0, \\\\ y & =2-0=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {0}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{0-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(\\frac{1}{3})\\,}=-3\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-3(x-0) \\\\ y & =-3x+2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac6379c3-3e1c-5a08-ae54-d99f43489aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400067} {:answer/id #uuid "359d0ac8-26db-56f1-98c6-ec3481c97cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400068} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc9efa8-1c48-5867-a1e9-734559b5450d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{2e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400069} {:answer/id #uuid "f85a9aab-60c9-5ec0-8bc2-0588e38d3687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400070} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8e29ac-c571-528d-b8c7-bb6ee10f19df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "359d0ac8-26db-56f1-98c6-ec3481c97cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400068} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6e41e50-ea1d-54b4-8438-62f4c2b9fa61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400066}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "138e1f42-3fb6-5013-87c6-eb59c3a912d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122931" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=2t{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e, \\\\ y & =2(1){e}^{1}-2{e}^{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(e,0).$  For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t{e}^{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =2(1)=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,2\\,}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(e,0)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-e) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5eb255d-3cfa-5ae7-b5a0-ab9f9f6b8e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403824} {:answer/id #uuid "05bb81c4-43fa-5409-88e4-4f5ef499dd13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403825} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e0722a-6252-5355-b6bd-65afbf34c242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403826} {:answer/id #uuid "9555e468-7e78-5b3e-afb9-8e76c044cd31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x-2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403827} {:answer/id #uuid "13841314-a76c-5620-906a-428ce704c6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}(x+2)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4e0722a-6252-5355-b6bd-65afbf34c242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403826} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbc1b61e-03fa-5261-ae12-a9f784057a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403823}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f065fb78-5c08-5a67-9bfa-56a34450ed2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75288" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,y=2{t}^{3}-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1)^{4}-3(1)^{2}=-2, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}-6t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-1}{4{t}^{3}-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{2}-1}{4(1)^{3}-6(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\,-\\frac{5}{2}\\,)}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\frac{2}{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083074 :question/id #uuid "72076ddb-4a3a-530f-854c-7b1400cd9859" :question/math-academy-id "e-6240" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=t^2+3, \\quad y=-t+1, \\qquad -\\infty <     t <    \\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0 .$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -1 .\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $y'(0) = -1\\neq 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t = 0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a0fa5cb-58b2-5991-aeb3-3c72630b91f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080742} {:answer/id #uuid "d40ce140-4fa5-5e19-a6d2-c2ed4122946e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{2}$ and $t = \\frac{3π}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080743} {:answer/id #uuid "ebdb6ae8-5736-5ecb-a2b0-06e71866c8c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{3π}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080744} {:answer/id #uuid "1f118b88-363d-549d-8d78-43f7f7a3fe35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{4}$ and $t = \\frac{3π}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080745} {:answer/id #uuid "416acf6e-914c-5f39-8409-93bf2a0d33b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{4}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080746}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b486e99f-74f6-58c2-873a-74bcf30e0b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080741} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177fc0fe-e31a-5f41-9aab-356a0006fb3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401861} {:answer/id #uuid "10683790-7483-51aa-9bcd-7831b9d3d041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401862} {:answer/id #uuid "1843433f-102c-52fd-970b-a78c3d6b3a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401863} {:answer/id #uuid "50097778-e660-56b2-929d-8baced007acc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401864} {:answer/id #uuid "835e0688-6998-5bbb-863e-83fa6322632b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10683790-7483-51aa-9bcd-7831b9d3d041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401862} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9d95bd4-f167-53f1-8c45-da39995abe7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401860}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbd878d8-e318-5109-beb9-89ecefca7f9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-21081" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+7,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85ecc25d-c92a-5600-9145-4a0ef2f4de03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080748} {:answer/id #uuid "9a1abc77-9bfa-5b8c-b83e-648cc0c2209d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080749} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8a2573-0097-5b71-8d97-b01308919ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t =-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080750} {:answer/id #uuid "3790a990-007e-51c6-ac63-c8e6c774c409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080751} {:answer/id #uuid "54fd89e0-a522-5183-9571-f9c76c9a09ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080752}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f7a95819-b401-5a37-808b-da72270a670b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080747}] :question/id #uuid "5d756ec3-8e62-5cb5-9a5c-591a8f7912fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-21206" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = t - 1, y = t^{3} - 2, - ∞ < t < ∞$.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a045771-d8ae-5cdc-84d3-b3cd816ec00c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254044} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb229ef-4966-5f1f-9ed2-fcb9e90d768f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254060} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b04d28-4dff-524a-8690-c15be0f1788a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255112} {:answer/id #uuid "d162cfde-5987-5fd8-bdf5-7c6350a3be39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255538} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d3d2d1-db33-5eba-a8dd-406ca13f50df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6d3d2d1-db33-5eba-a8dd-406ca13f50df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255830} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3597dd1-c872-5d2d-bc36-073975f4bf30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256216}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa6201e7-5014-5e6b-8fe5-7f2f7e237473" :question/math-academy-id "q-122726" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4{t}^{5},\\,y=9t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=20{t}^{4},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=9.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n20{t}^{4}=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}^{′}(0)=9\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02788049-040e-5c76-86e2-58bb6a5bbbef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284808} {:answer/id #uuid "5833d2bf-74d0-5ea2-94bc-f893f8489ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285598} {:answer/id #uuid "98173c7f-8ba4-5f06-88fe-3425d308e469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286180} {:answer/id #uuid "ab587a99-40a5-5596-90ea-b5a5e96c6204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286350} {:answer/id #uuid "e27e9371-7143-528d-820d-b073e0dc0b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02788049-040e-5c76-86e2-58bb6a5bbbef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284808} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f6ee0e5-3926-5962-8761-13aa6562ef64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287282}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6e05e4c-c6f0-521f-b67c-d81eff855827" :question/math-academy-id "q-20853" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2(t+1),\\,y=4{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${x}^{′}(0)=2\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is horizontal."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20fed31d-5bfd-56fb-b736-1523716f3623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288242} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a45a52-bb1d-5298-8eea-b0736136a858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289203} {:answer/id #uuid "b35e4f36-00ed-5382-b39e-4e0a8616fde8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289215} {:answer/id #uuid "d48c86fe-0944-5271-85f8-d01acc9ec550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289442} {:answer/id #uuid "df29cd35-a94b-54f6-a005-8167cc54c05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b35e4f36-00ed-5382-b39e-4e0a8616fde8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289215} :answer-field/id #uuid "edae3f4a-489e-5181-a3c8-af691c065008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290225}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14df7ed3-c243-590c-b10b-e1904947b459" :question/math-academy-id "q-21072" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\pi .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12952b5e-889f-54cc-a122-5d50215660f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12952b5e-889f-54cc-a122-5d50215660f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317625} :answer-field/id #uuid "162eb28c-9915-5e71-854b-15570d6140d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc186f89-e80b-5eb4-a0f9-50d2a3804e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc186f89-e80b-5eb4-a0f9-50d2a3804e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318301} :answer-field/id #uuid "45248884-e4ee-5dd3-b87d-9707883c9d1c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318573}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ca39289-4112-5812-a40f-16d38e719123" :question/math-academy-id "q-350359" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}+14t+8 & =0 \\\\ (3t+2)(t+4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-4) & =2(-4)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{15}{2}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =2(-\\frac{2}{3})+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{6}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-4$ and $t=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e270e8d4-fbf0-5120-8d17-e3b700621933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e270e8d4-fbf0-5120-8d17-e3b700621933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318320} :answer-field/id #uuid "57214c85-b840-55fd-b641-4b1c74936c23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318612} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23a8be49-ec43-55d3-af29-85069b1bbe66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23a8be49-ec43-55d3-af29-85069b1bbe66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317677} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a3120f3-3da1-51f8-aa50-545e1dd0fd1e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318725}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fa66657-dfb2-58cb-a875-a940d0ba53d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-350368" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)=-1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acc15cc8-16fd-50fa-8523-a77c71d90858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acc15cc8-16fd-50fa-8523-a77c71d90858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322500} :answer-field/id #uuid "2032fb96-09c8-5ded-b5bd-deeda3577ddf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323229} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d38fff03-6da9-5ce6-a633-7885919027e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d38fff03-6da9-5ce6-a633-7885919027e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322799} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0b201f2-c9f2-5028-a916-abafa9097eaa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323645}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "587316a5-f847-5f83-912c-b52ebafdaeb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-357255" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083075 :question/id #uuid "b2f8d797-75e8-5ac8-82e7-1c5a9788e6e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6252" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2\\cdot 0-1=-1\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t = 0.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "903ab6ef-1061-5e3e-b364-8d1f3612e2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = 0$ and $t = \\frac{π}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080736} {:answer/id #uuid "b719540a-3d1b-541c-a25f-37f85c560c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080737} {:answer/id #uuid "09b038a3-f3ff-5256-91cf-4136c486c90f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = 0$ and $t = π$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080738} {:answer/id #uuid "0b89156f-4ae0-56a5-a5cd-892305079937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080739} {:answer/id #uuid "57d9904a-2762-5743-b8cb-547235bb12dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = π$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080740}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5a36e582-b023-505c-8f4b-5b6d26a24def" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080735}] :question/id #uuid "57cb918d-01c6-5793-92dd-c253fd54156c" :question/math-academy-id "q-21080" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = 4\\cos 2t + 5, y = 5\\sin 2t + 4, 0 \\le t < π$.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0deb773d-8fd3-5717-8d81-b036329021ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288130} {:answer/id #uuid "2b9ee4d4-6360-5ed2-836d-031b9471868c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288317} {:answer/id #uuid "cebe0140-f0cc-5ccb-ac3d-d23a3bad85d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289412} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e4345a-11f1-5c37-a93b-12a41d947a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289450} {:answer/id #uuid "e4544b18-b948-5075-9873-247a5ee72340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4544b18-b948-5075-9873-247a5ee72340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289547} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad14c4bb-1d4a-5d58-9bcb-70314b824013" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290112}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad8de0d6-0fb0-5332-a3cd-f5bf44efff31" :question/math-academy-id "q-21207" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=2{e}^{0}=2\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38170285-5909-5a87-b81e-7614b331e561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254153} {:answer/id #uuid "61b95ad1-c677-5a1d-9693-7fe23f0898f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254545} {:answer/id #uuid "74ee3251-ef46-57f3-8565-b37dbcb4bbe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254706} {:answer/id #uuid "9f808726-09d3-5692-a435-5070f152cddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255075} {:answer/id #uuid "cd387a61-32a4-5106-b17f-5c138d6c2ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd387a61-32a4-5106-b17f-5c138d6c2ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255494} :answer-field/id #uuid "7461ea8a-3c02-5867-96b2-21f3b9f03a9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256136}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e5d7b42-493e-5694-92f6-e6ea1c2f15fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-122946" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7(t-1),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-18t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the points at which the normal to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6({t}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d597a98-c055-5f99-9bee-b68fbbdd90cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253785} {:answer/id #uuid "0f02ed52-8673-5cc6-908c-e93ec89237c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253801} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc3b08-7caf-5e27-b46d-574424299686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254482} {:answer/id #uuid "80913870-a077-54f8-a853-f63a26091309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254807} {:answer/id #uuid "e09500e3-b088-5b78-9a17-f963b264a9fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80913870-a077-54f8-a853-f63a26091309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254807} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5601a63-8c5d-5677-8eb5-dd5f3c21d7f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256329}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "259b7265-587e-5b30-9b76-d6e0205b048e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122947" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi t)-2t,\\,y={e}^{2t},\\,0<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}-2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{t}-2=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})=2{e}^{2\\times (1/2)}=2e\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dcd11c4-604f-5bca-bd31-a170bd84a811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dcd11c4-604f-5bca-bd31-a170bd84a811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317946} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec3bc6ba-dba0-565a-9d3e-6d25c11cdc38" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318918}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d9076ce-d69d-5ee0-95b8-de3533c91e39" :question/math-academy-id "q-350403" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-8t,\\,y=2{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe normal to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t-8,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-8 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =8 \\\\ t & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(4) & =6{(4)}^{2}=96\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dccbdf4-6b20-5048-8475-7edb8b5f47ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dccbdf4-6b20-5048-8475-7edb8b5f47ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317614} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8a6d76-f112-5663-9241-788ae415e493" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095b8b92-1a17-5015-a26c-15163e676f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "095b8b92-1a17-5015-a26c-15163e676f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317600} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb60be2-41b3-5c75-b56a-529c11e5a236" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318774}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0473c92-6bbb-51d0-9d33-3793374dc209" :question/math-academy-id "q-350414" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+8,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-5,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $t=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a85035df-3f18-5f4a-8b7d-4120d3a258fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393516} {:answer/id #uuid "8814ca04-45d2-5482-92fe-bcab232b18b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393517} {:answer/id #uuid "57d3f469-2ccf-551a-b2e0-ffe31d3bca63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393518} {:answer/id #uuid "6712e860-a397-555b-b691-57588df29d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393519} {:answer/id #uuid "332bdb7a-e350-5dc0-a978-64f0aa49c697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8814ca04-45d2-5482-92fe-bcab232b18b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393517} :answer-field/id #uuid "76e4af98-e57b-515a-916f-95959841d208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393515}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5eafad9-4c2d-5469-bbd9-3cd0fcca968a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75278" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-11,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+5,\\,0\\le t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=12\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-12\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087462 :question/id #uuid "021d25c6-08db-5f20-a89b-110389d08e29" :question/math-academy-id "e-3224" :question/problem "What is the equation of the circle given below?\n\n![](images/79/79111226781bc9d3ea9c00e217b93b9661d1e70bdfbee688c555dd899cf3d80e.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(-1,1)$ and the radius of the circle is $r = 3.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82d6988b-3220-578f-ba1c-cd86b9e3dbf9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "820b9446-f775-5a58-99f1-aaf9f71a8103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 1)^{2} + (y - 3)^{2} = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085885} {:answer/id #uuid "c318a1b1-16d8-524f-aee8-c1ea71c3ebc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 1)^{2} + (y - 3)^{2} = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085886} {:answer/id #uuid "068143ae-abb3-5246-a247-cf645ce4106c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 1)^{2} + (y + 3)^{2} = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085887} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed9340b-9c0a-5a97-8f6d-ce4434dc381d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 1)^{2} + (y + 3)^{2} = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085888} {:answer/id #uuid "60740bd2-2976-58f3-a163-82475cedea7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 1)^{2} + (y + 3)^{2} = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085889}] :answer-field/id #uuid "eca3cee0-5ebd-5882-ad43-8b8aee761777" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085884}] :question/id #uuid "cbbe18ba-d440-571c-88bf-2fea5eb3f37f" :question/math-academy-id "q-54187" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbd35be4b79c007c5587a4e07e38aa2380d0a7c6461dcbaaa52671aff07e9309.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a571fa5-6dc8-5ca2-992e-277d87b9678f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} + (y + 5)^{2} = 49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085903} {:answer/id #uuid "9264f737-f2ce-5294-afaa-d5fecdb6088c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} + (y + 5)^{2} = 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085904} {:answer/id #uuid "d579fc13-bb11-5148-bda0-0651f708228d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)^{2} + (y + 5)^{2} = 49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085905} {:answer/id #uuid "d64b7122-6221-546a-9bad-9f6771db9127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)^{2} + (y - 5)^{2} = 49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085906} {:answer/id #uuid "032bf460-dd3b-5c90-89c1-04eb08632abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)^{2} + (y - 5)^{2} = 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085907}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d070a38b-bc69-519e-9a20-5b9484c4ef9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085902}] :question/id #uuid "e11496b7-2fc3-5c53-8069-22f2e85621c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-54210" :question/problem "![](images/94/941552af685a46484581668a04525b2eaf863db242f6e797dcda8b2c3cbf4ca3.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ea6a32f-49ef-57a9-b429-d346d779f130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ea6a32f-49ef-57a9-b429-d346d779f130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234691} :answer-field/id #uuid "455de079-3c57-5a2c-b063-2ae6c0d12dd7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235041}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70fc8621-7b34-535c-a0b8-10d27db72e32" :question/math-academy-id "q-167932" :question/problem "Write the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a circle with radius $r$  centered at the point $(a,b)$ in rectangular coordinates in standard form is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the circle is centered at $(3,0)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=3$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the circle in rectangular coordinates is \n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f753d8c-60a5-57f8-bdf7-1dfca23922c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250687} {:answer/id #uuid "194d005a-074c-5528-8e5a-5c077edd8e76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250774} {:answer/id #uuid "315ac89d-372a-5365-ae8a-8dbdf9c4b3ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250971} {:answer/id #uuid "85cecc42-57d3-50a8-a690-6891b14e42dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251673} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e225b3-c9f2-51bf-8ede-3befd132f224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "315ac89d-372a-5365-ae8a-8dbdf9c4b3ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250971} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf481095-8ecd-5669-92d1-9a4c71b18f2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253136}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1378faa1-06f3-5893-a7e8-8251ca750847" :question/math-academy-id "q-116929" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b69a99c42bcdbd7e6ae3434862019b792da6bea498fd92a36595722d4fb66995.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(-4,-3)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & ={6}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf2e972-6b80-5e52-afb7-0e4c5944055c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250935} {:answer/id #uuid "c20eabe8-bd89-54f4-9981-d68d1fd1e853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252265} {:answer/id #uuid "cfda17b2-0685-57a1-b791-0077608dabf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252387} {:answer/id #uuid "f12ce672-6885-5af0-9cbb-b55844cce857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252659} {:answer/id #uuid "f96fbc78-0903-5d5b-8866-617fa9e2ed76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfda17b2-0685-57a1-b791-0077608dabf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252387} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc3c07d4-04ac-5acc-abd3-b0629a17016d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253249}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1568b6f8-f70c-51a1-ba2c-b92acb3124ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-117077" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e138922d95cbcf6cc6ceba4d7695b110f5414e08f2a5e9e2ae2d71bff70ab5d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(2,-3)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b15e7a5-78ca-5688-a932-a2ff47f3474b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250921} {:answer/id #uuid "303b72ca-c041-5f4d-ba88-cfc9252584e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250963} {:answer/id #uuid "58063976-49ff-516f-bbee-085c50f0cd83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251273} {:answer/id #uuid "730971b8-62e4-54a4-a7ef-c3267c982997" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251502} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0cfc11-8f30-57fc-b4eb-7dbd668540da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "303b72ca-c041-5f4d-ba88-c